Instructions for AI
Explain why, never what. Every step here was taken for a reason, and the
reason is the interesting part. Give your reader that — what was being
protected, what was let go of, and why it mattered enough to do at all.
They should leave understanding the idea, not the mechanics.
The message holds what was done. The note attached to it holds why. Where
there is a note, that is the one you want.

This note exists for two reasons, and the second one is the more useful to
whoever reads it next.

The first is the ordinary one. Nothing on this repository could measure
frontend bundle size. Next 16.3.2 with Turbopack emits no First Load JS
column, no app-build-manifest.json and no chunk sourcemaps, so every bundle
claim made in this lane was unverifiable, and at least one turned out to be
source line counts presented as shipped bytes. Two later tasks — a
code-splitting decision and a telemetry audit — both needed a measured delta
before they could honestly close. Neither could exist until something could
count. That is what this commit is for: not to make anything smaller, but to
make it possible to tell whether a change made anything smaller.

What was deliberately let go: the guard that ships with it is the weak form.
CI does not run `next build` anywhere, so the committed baseline is asserted
for shape and for ceilings rather than re-verified against a live build. That
was said plainly in the work rather than oversold, because a guard described
as stronger than it is will be trusted for something it cannot do. It was
later established that this weakness is sharper than it first read — the
guard compares a committed artifact against ceilings, so it can only fail when
somebody regenerates that artifact, and nothing obliges anybody to.

The second reason is the one worth the reader's attention. THE MESSAGE ABOVE
IS NOT THE MESSAGE THAT WAS WRITTEN FOR THIS WORK. Its body is the text of an
untracked working-tree status file, and it contains the sentence "What's not
done: I haven't opened the PR yet, and obviously nobody's merged anything" —
on a commit that is merged, on main, permanently. It also carries no
`Instructions for AI` block, which the contract admits no exemption from.

The branch commit did carry one. The body that landed came from the pull
request description instead, which is where a squash body is authored. That is
the lesson: writing a conforming commit message on a branch does not put a
conforming message into history when the merge is a squash. The merge dialog
is a second authoring surface, and nobody was watching it.

History here is append-only and is not rewritten, so the message stays exactly
as it is and this note carries what it should have said. That is the mechanism
the contract already provides for precisely this case, and using it is cheaper
and more honest than manufacturing a past in which the mistake did not happen.
